DB maintainers\n\nSource\n    www.sciencedirect.com ↗\n\nPublic record\n    R932\n\nStable alias\n    berge-fulkerson-conjecture-claim-status-20260731\n\nProjection\n    Reproduction fields are derived from the immutable record.\n\nA statement this project treats as settled at the recorded evidence grade, with the work that backs it.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBerge-Fulkerson Covers, Cycle Double Covers, Flows and Exact Normality Defects of the First Counterexamples to the Petersen Coloring Conjecture | Zenodo (https://zenodo.org/records/22835152)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: yesterday; # Berge-Fulkerson Covers, Cycle Double Covers, Flows and Exact Normality Defects of the First Counterexamples to the Petersen Coloring Conjecture ... Jaeger's Petersen coloring conjecture (1988), which asserted that every bridgeless cubic graph admits an edge map onto the Petersen graph sending every vertex star onto a vertex star, was refuted in August 2026: Putman exhibited two nonisomorphic 112-vertex counterexamples with SAT-solver certificates, Jooken gave a human-checkable proof, and Goedgebeur, Jooken, Máčajová, Mattiolo and Mazzuoccolo found a 52-vertex counterexample together with infinite families, placing the smallest counterexample between 38 and 52 vertices.\n\nPublished September 18, 2026 | Version 0.03\n\nPreprint Open\n\n# Berge-Fulkerson Covers, Cycle Double Covers, Flows and Exact Normality Defects of the First Counterexamples to the Petersen Coloring Conjecture\n\n### Authors/Creators\n\n  * Santibáñez-Leal, Felipe^{1}\n\n  * 1. CAOS open-research programme, Santiago, Chile\n\n## Description\n\nJaeger's Petersen coloring conjecture (1988), which asserted that every bridgeless cubic graph admits an edge map onto the Petersen graph sending every vertex star onto a vertex star, was refuted in August 2026: Putman exhibited two nonisomorphic 112-vertex counterexamples with SAT-solver certificates, Jooken gave a human-checkable proof, and Goedgebeur, Jooken, Máčajová, Mattiolo and Mazzuoccolo found a 52-vertex counterexample together with infinite families, placing the smallest counterexample between 38 and 52 vertices. The conjecture had been the strongest known sufficient condition for the Berge-Fulkerson conjecture and the 5-cycle double cover conjecture.\nA parity argument explains the first half in general: for any edge map into the Petersen graph the number of bad vertices is never exactly one, so every counterexample has defect at least 2 and the three known counterexamples are extremal. Finally, we prove that no counterexample consists solely of copies of the Petersen 4-pole F from which all known counterexamples are built, and report a counterexample-guided search over compositions of F with free vertices below 52 vertices. All computations are propositional with checked DRAT certificates or explicit witnesses. The paper decides nothing about the general conjectures.\n\nSource code, data, computational records and verification instructions: https://github.com/fsantibanezleal/CAOS_RESEARCH (problems/combinatorics/petersen-coloring).\n\n## Other\n\nVersion notes. The author name is printed as Santibáñez-Leal, as on the author's ORCID record (0000-0002-0150-3246). The content is unchanged.\n\n## Files\n\npetersen-coloring-consequence-audit-v0.03.pdf\n\nFiles (356.0 kB)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Lower Bound for Kruskal's Weak Tree Function tree(3) (arXiv:2601.19658v1) - ScienceStack (https://www.sciencestack.ai/paper/2601.19658)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last month; It introduces an explicit sequence of unlabeled rooted trees and a detailed leg-elimination analysis, culminating in the Leg Elimination Formula $L(x) = 6 \\cdot 2^{x} - 2x - 6$ and the concrete bound $tree(3) \\ge 844{,}424{,}930{,}131{,}960 = 3 \\cdot 2^{48} - 8$. ... We establish an explicit lower bound for Kruskal's weak tree function at n=3, proving that tree(3) >= 844,424,930,131,960 = 3 * 2^48 - 8.\n\n# A Lower Bound for Kruskal's Weak Tree Function tree(3)\n\nMark Giroux\n\n## TL;DR\n\nThis work addresses the problem of establishing a nontrivial lower bound for Kruskal's weak tree function by focusing on tree(3). It introduces an explicit sequence of unlabeled rooted trees and a detailed leg-elimination analysis, culminating in the Leg Elimination Formula $L(x) = 6 \\cdot 2^{x} - 2x - 6$ and the concrete bound $tree(3) \\ge 844{,}424{,}930{,}131{,}960 = 3 \\cdot 2^{48} - 8$. The main result demonstrates rapid growth of the weak tree function even for small arguments and discusses its implications for tree(4) and the fast-growing hierarchy. The paper situates the bound among known values, compares with prior estimates, and highlights open questions about tightness and broader consequences in combinatorial proof theory.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe establish an explicit lower bound for Kruskal's weak tree function at n=3, proving that tree(3) >= 844,424,930,131,960 = 3 * 2^48 - 8. This is achieved by constructing an explicit sequence of unlabeled rooted trees satisfying the constraints of the weak tree function and carefully analyzing the combinatorics of the \"leg elimination\" process. Our bound significantly exceeds previous estimates and demonstrates that even for small arguments, the weak tree function exhibits rapid growth.\n\n# A Lower Bound for Kruskal's Weak Tree Function tree(3)\n\nPublished: January 27, 2026arXiv: 2601.19658v1View on Semantic ScholarCite\n\n## TL;DR\n\nThis work addresses the problem of establishing a nontrivial lower bound for Kruskal's weak tree function by focusing on tree(3). It introduces an explicit sequence of unlabeled rooted trees and a detailed leg-elimination analysis, culminating in the Leg Elimination Formula\n\nand the concrete bound\n\n. The main result demonstrates rapid growth of the weak tree function even for small arguments and discusses its implications for tree(4) and the fast-growing hierarchy. The paper situates the bound among known values, compares with prior estimates, and highlights open questions about tightness and broader consequences in combinatorial proof theory.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe establish an explicit lower bound for Kruskal's weak tree function at n=3, proving that tree(3) >= 844,424,930,131,960 = 3 * 2^48 - 8. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBILL, RECORD LECTURE!!!! (https://www.cs.umd.edu/users/gasarch/COURSES/752/S26/slides/wqofasttalk.pdf)\nciteturn7search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; tree(3) ≥844, 424, 930, 181, 960\nWhat is Known About tree(n)?\nKnown:\ntree(1) = 1\ntree(2) = 5\ntree(3) ≥844, 424, 930, 181, 960\ntree(4) ≫GN (Grahams Number)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFulkerson Conjecture -- from Wolfram MathWorld (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/FulkersonConjecture.html)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Fulkerson Conjecture ... B 150, 144-176, 2021. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jctb.2021.04.004. ... \"The Equivalence of Two Conjectures of Berge and Fulkerson.\"\n\n# Fulkerson Conjecture\n\n* * *\n\nThe Fulkerson conjecture states that every bridgeless cubic graph has a collection of six perfect matchings such that each edge belongs to exactly two of them. Mazzuoccolo (2011) showed that this is equivalent to Berge's conjecture that every bridgeless cubic graph should have perfect matching cover index at most 5.\n\nThe Fulkerson conjecture is a dual form of the cycle double cover conjecture. Cubic graphs with perfect matching cover index at least 5 are therefore important test objects for this and related conjectures (Máčajová and Škoviera 2021).\n\nThe now-refuted Petersen coloring conjecture would have implied the Fulkerson conjecture. Its counterexamples do not refute this weaker conjecture.\n\n## See also\n\nBerge Perfect Matching Conjecture, Bridgeless Graph, Cycle Double Cover Conjecture, Perfect Matching, Perfect Matching Cover Index, Petersen Coloring Conjecture, Snark\n\n## References\n\nMáčajová, E. and Škoviera, M. \"Cubic Graphs That Cannot Be Covered with Four Perfect Matchings.\" J. Combin. Th. Ser. B 150, 144-176, 2021. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jctb.2021.04.004.Mazzuoccolo, G. \"The Equivalence of Two Conjectures of Berge and Fulkerson.\" J. Graph Th. 68, 125-128, 2011.\n\n## Cite this as:\n\nWeisstein, Eric W. \"Fulkerson Conjecture.\" From MathWorld--A Wolfram Resource. https://mathworld.wolfram.com/FulkersonConjecture.html--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThree-point bound certification of the snub cube as an optimal spherical code in R3 (https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/snub-cube-three-point-bounds-optimality)\nciteturn7search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Show that the Bachoc–Vallentin three-point semidefinite programming bounds certify that the configuration of the 24 vertices of a snub cube is an optimal spherical code on S^2, matching the known geometric proof of optimality.\n\n# Three-point bound certification of the snub cube as an optimal spherical code in R3\n\nShow that the Bachoc–Vallentin three-point semidefinite programming bounds certify that the configuration of the 24 vertices of a snub cube is an optimal spherical code on S^2, matching the known geometric proof of optimality.\n\n### Background\n\nThe authors obtain a three-point bound proof for the 9-point optimal code in R3 and conjecture the same approach should verify the 24-point snub cube, which is already known to be optimal by geometric arguments due to Robinson.\n\nA semidefinite programming proof would provide an independent certification and illustrate further sharpness of three-point bounds in low-dimensional cases.\n\n### References\n\nConjecture 1.4. Three-point bounds prove that the 24 vertices of a snub cube are an optimal spherical code in three dimensions.\n\n— Optimality of spherical codes via exact semidefinite programming bounds  (2403.16874 - Cohn et al., 2024) in Conjecture 1.4, Section 1.3\n\n### Related Problems\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThey say tree(3) is 844 but I got 10^300 (https://www.reddit.com/r/learnmath/comments/1lmu5mx)\nciteturn7reddit13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: 1.3 years ago; Not to confused with upper case TREE(3). ... idk man but it looks like 844,424,930,131,960 is a lower bound and has not been proven to be equal to tree(3).\n|1,050|((511)(511))||66,847,909|((501)(500)||78,855,825,974,236|((490)(490))|\n|1,051|((510)(541))||66,847,910|((500)(66847410)||78,855,825,974,237|((78855825973747)(489))|\n|1,081|((510)(511))||133,694,820|((500)(500)||157,711,651,947,494|((490)(489))|\n|1,082|((572)(510))||133,694,821|((499)(133694322)||157,711,651,947,495|((489)(157711651947006))|\n|1,144|((510)(510))||267,388,643|((499)(500)||315,423,303,894,012|((489)(489))|\n|1,145|((509)(635))||267,388,644|((267388145)(499)||315,423,303,894,013|((488)(315423303893525))|\n|1,270|((509)(510))||534,776,290|((499)(499)||630,846,607,787,049|((488)(489))|\n|1,271|((762)(509))||534,776,291|((498)(534775792)||630,846,607,787,050|((630846607786562)(488))|\n|1,524|((509)(509))||1,069,551,584|((498)(499)||1,261,693,215,573,120|((488)(488))|\n|1,525|((508)(1,017))||1,069,551,585|((1069551087)(498)||||\n\n\n\nI thought maybe symmetrical trees are forbidden and considered embed, like T8 and T10 for example, in which case i could draw only 2040 trees, but i see no reason why would it be forbidden\n\n[Saturday June 28 2025] [+37 votes]\nbro you need help\n\n  [Saturday June 28 2025] [+8 votes]\n  Image: img\n  sorry its distorted in the post, here is the image\n\n[Saturday June 28 2025] [+31 votes]\nCheck your carbon monoxide alarms\n\n  [Saturday June 28 2025] [+26 vote