 Finally we show how to build a ribbon graph, and for some special o6c4c cases we show that this ribbon graph corresponds to an oriented 6-cycle double cover. Github: this https URL\n\nComments:  | v3 updates: renamed series name, fixed typos, fixed references, added updates about new proofs\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2501.05348 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2501.05348v3 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.05348 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\nFrom: Nikolay Ulyanov [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 9 Jan 2025 16:22:15 UTC (313 KB)\n[v2] Sun, 12 Jan 2025 19:18:03 UTC (330 KB)\n[v3] Tue, 24 Mar 2026 15:26:15 UTC (286 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRUP - Cycle separating cuts in possible counterexamples to the cycle double cover and the Berge-Fulkerson conjectures (https://repozitorij.upr.si/IzpisGradiva.php?id=22692&lang=eng&print=&prip=rul%3A28701987%3Ad1)\nciteturn7search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Abstract: It is known that smallest counterexamples to the Cycle Double Cover Conjecture and Berge-Fulkerson Conjecture (if they exist) are cyclically 4- and 5-edge-connected, respectively. ... [Select]: MÁČAJOVÁ, Edita, MAZZUOCCOLO, Giuseppe and TABARELLI, Gloria, 2026, Cycle separating cuts in possible counterexamples to the cycle double cover and the Berge-Fulkerson conjectures.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n巨大数の樹海へようこそ！ (https://www.math.mi.i.nagoya-u.ac.jp/~kihara/pdf/teach/tree-final.pdf)\nciteturn7search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Crawled: 8 months ago; Additional scattered text near the middle includes “TREE(3) > tree(tree(3)) > tree(844 )”, “tree”, “TREE(3)”, “TREE(3)”, and a green bracketed reference “[1]”.</visual_element>\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn some perfect matching conjectures in infin